## Optional patterns (adapt to your setup)

The patterns below are powerful when you've set up the underlying systems. They don't ship in the kit because they require infrastructure you'd build yourself. Pick the ones that fit your workflow — or skip the section entirely.

**Meta-rule first:** When you have a system for *where things go*, tell Claude about it explicitly. The more Claude knows about your filing system — your Notion structure, your Drive folder taxonomy, your project repo conventions — the more it can put things away properly without asking. Be generous with location-routing instructions.

**Suggested patterns:**

- **Project planning files.** If every major project has a `PLANNING.md` (or `handoff.md`) at the repo root, tell Claude to read it on re-entry and update it at session end. Compounds across multi-session work — you don't re-explain context every time.

- **Brand tokens.** If you maintain a brand-identity file (palette, typography, voice rules), tell Claude to load it before producing any branded artifact. Prevents palette and typography invention drift.

- **Inter-AI messaging.** If Claude.ai and Claude Code (or other AIs) talk via a relay channel — a Notion database, shared file, or similar — tell Claude to check the inbox at session start.

- **Multi-surface delegation.** If you use multiple AI surfaces (Claude.ai for strategy, Perplexity for research, Claude Code for implementation, etc.), tell Claude which work routes where. Otherwise it'll try to handle everything itself when delegation would be faster.

- **Augmentation prompts.** If you regularly ask things like "what didn't I think of?" or "how would this be better?" — tell Claude to address one or two of those proactively in research and analysis output, not just when explicitly asked.

